NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
asz
- the micronix Z80 assembler
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
asz [ -m98l ] [ -o file ] [ source ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Asz reads Z80 assembly and writes a relocatable object in Whitesmith's
format, the one ld links.  For a source file foo.s it writes foo.o;
reading the standard input it writes a.out.  It is the pass ccc runs
after c1, so most of what it assembles comes straight out of the
compiler, but it reads hand-written assembly the same way.
.sp
A program is three segments, text, data and bss, switched with the
pseudo-ops below.  Symbols are fifteen characters, or nine with -m.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-m and -9	Use nine-character symbol names instead of fifteen, the old Whitesmith limit, for an object that must match one made that way.

.ti -10
-8	Do not relax a jp into a jr.  The 8080 has no jr, so this keeps the output 8080-compatible.

.ti -10
-l	Write a listing file, foo.lst beside foo.o.

.ti -10
-o file	Name the object file rather than taking it from the source or a.out.

.ti -10
-n	No timeout.  On the host this turns off a five-second hang guard; here it is accepted and does nothing.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PSEUDO-OPS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
...text, .data, .bss	Switch to the text, data or bss segment.  psect text, data or bss is the HiTech-C spelling of the same thing.

.ti -10
...globl name	Make name visible to the linker; .global and a bare global do the same, and several names may follow, separated by commas.

.ti -10
...extern name	Declare name defined in another object; several names may follow.

.ti -10
...defb n or "s"	Emit a byte, a run of bytes, or the bytes of a string.  .db and a bare defb are the same.

.ti -10
...defw n	Emit a sixteen-bit word; .dw and defw are the same.

.ti -10
...defl n	Emit a thirty-two-bit long; .dl is the same.

.ti -10
...ds n	Reserve n bytes of space; defs is the same.

.ti -10
name = expr and name equ expr	Give name the value of the expression, absolute or segment-relative.

.ti -10
name:	Define name as a label at the current address; name:: also exports it.

.ti -10
N:	A numeric local label; a reference to Nf means the next one forward, Nb the last one backward.

.ti -10
...error text	Print text and stop.  The compiler's later pass uses it to put a real message where an "unknown directive" would otherwise be.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The pseudo-op set is deliberately small, and a page written for
another assembler will miss some of what it expects.  There is no
...org, no .include, no .if or .endif, no macros, no .ascii or .asciz -
a string goes in a .defb - no .comm, and the data names are the
Whitesmith .defb, .defw and .defl rather than .byte, .word and .long.
.sp
Every object asz writes carries two symbols, .edata and .ebss.  They
are not pseudo-ops and never appear in source: the linker reads them
to merge the same uninitialised global defined in forty objects, and
the leading dot is what keeps them from ever colliding with a C
identifier.
.sp
A jp whose target is near is shortened to a jr automatically, which is
what makes the compiler's output fit; -8 turns that off.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
